    {
      "file": "items/cex-knot-kt-47-boundary-case.md",
      "item_id": "cex-knot-kt-47-boundary-case",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The declared crossing count $c=1$ and the one-entry word $-1$.\n\n[F1] [[def-planar-diagram-gauss-and-dowker-codes]]: for each crossing label $j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,c\\}$, a Gauss code contains exactly two visits to $j$, one over and one under.\n\n[F2] [[alg-planar-code-validation-and-diagram-reconstruction]]: for a nonempty Gauss input, after range checking, the procedure counts the two over/under visits and returns `invalid-crossing-visit-count` at the first label whose counts are not $(1,1)$, before any graph or face test.\n\n[F3] [[thm-planar-code-validation-and-diagram-reconstruction-procedure-properties]]: each invalid return is the first failed predicate in the stated procedure order.\n\n\n\n1.1 The only declared label is $1$, which lies in $\\{1\\}$. The word has one entry, and its negative sign makes that entry an under-visit. Hence its visit-count pair is $(0,1)$, not the required $(1,1)$ in [F1]. [given, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 The range check passes, so the validator reaches its Gauss visit-count check. By [F2] and [F3] it returns `invalid-crossing-visit-count` with $(0,1)$ before constructing a crossing graph or computing faces. Therefore mere presence of every declared label does not make a signed word a Gauss code. [given, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1] ∎"
    },
    {
      "file": "items/ex-knot-kt-47-worked-cases.md",
      "item_id": "ex-knot-kt-47-worked-cases",
      "status": "repair",
      "strategy": "direct",
      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** The based signed Gauss word $-1,+3,-2,+1,-3,+2$, the sign vector $(+1,+1,+1)$, and the page's normalized dart and face conventions.\n\n[F1] [[def-planar-diagram-gauss-and-dowker-codes]]: a positive Gauss visit is over and a negative visit is under; each crossing occurs once over and once under; a positive normalized crossing has clockwise record $(O_{\\rm out},U_{\\rm in},O_{\\rm in},U_{\\rm out})$; face cycles are those of $\\alpha\\circ\\sigma$.\n\n[F2] [[def-oriented-crossing-sign-writhe-and-blackboard-framing]]: the algebraic sign is $\\operatorname{sgn}\\det(v_{\\rm over},v_{\\rm under})$, and the positive Artin half twist has sign $+1$.\n\n[F3] [[lem-planar-diagram-code-realizability-and-face-validation]]: a nonempty connected code with consistent local crossing data has a spherical realization when $V-E+F=2$, and a chosen face gives its plane model.\n\n[F4] [[thm-planar-code-validation-and-diagram-reconstruction-procedure-properties]]: the validator accepts exactly codes satisfying the incidence, strand, connectedness, face-count, and outer-face predicates and returns their crossing-disk/edge-band model.\n\n[F5] [[thm-enumerate-diagrams-up-to-crossing-bound-procedure-properties]]: the enumerator includes every normalized connected one-component code up to the specified crossing bound that passes validation.\n\n[F6] [Fish, “Codes, Links and Racks,” §5.2.1, p. 67](https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/136332/1/WRAP_Theses_Fish_2001.pdf): the right trefoil signed Gauss code is $o1+,u2+,o3+,u1+,o2+,u3+$, and crossing signs are unchanged between the two visits to a crossing.\n\n\n\n1.1 The visits of the given word are, in order, under at crossing $1$, over at $3$, under at $2$, over at $1$, under at $3$, and over at $2$. The source code in [F6], started at its fourth visit, is $u1+,o2+,u3+,o1+,u2+,o3+$; exchanging labels $2$ and $3$ gives exactly the stated word and the same positive sign at each crossing. Thus the word is the stated based traversal of the right trefoil. [given, F1, F6, algebra]\n\n1.2 Write $j:e_k$ for the dart labelled by edge $e_k$ at crossing $j$. Applying $\\phi=\\alpha\\circ\\sigma$ gives\n\n2.1 Let $x_i$ be the directed edge from visit $i$ to visit $i+1$, with indices modulo $6$. Then $x_1$ joins $1:U_{\\rm out}$ to $3:O_{\\rm in}$, $x_2$ joins $3:O_{\\rm out}$ to $2:U_{\\rm in}$, $x_3$ joins $2:U_{\\rm out}$ to $1:O_{\\rm in}$, $x_4$ joins $1:O_{\\rm out}$ to $3:U_{\\rm in}$, $x_5$ joins $3:U_{\\rm out}$ to $2:O_{\\rm in}$, and $x_6$ joins $2:O_{\\rm out}$ to $1:U_{\\rm in}$. At each crossing the sign is positive by the given vector and [F2]. Reading each crossing clockwise from its outgoing over-dart therefore gives $X_1=(x_4,x_6,x_3,x_1)$, $X_2=(x_6,x_2,x_5,x_3)$, and $X_3=(x_2,x_4,x_1,x_5)$. Scanning crossing $1$ then $2$ then $3$ assigns canonical edge names $e_1=x_4,e_2=x_6,e_3=x_3,e_4=x_1,e_5=x_2,e_6=x_5$, yielding the three normalized records in the statement. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, construct]\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1:e_1&\\mapsto2:e_2\\mapsto3:e_5\\mapsto1:e_1,\\\\\n1:e_2&\\mapsto2:e_3\\mapsto1:e_2,\\\\\n1:e_3&\\mapsto3:e_4\\mapsto2:e_6\\mapsto1:e_3,\\\\\n1:e_4&\\mapsto3:e_1\\mapsto1:e_4,\\\\\n2:e_5&\\mapsto3:e_6\\mapsto2:e_5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor example, $1:e_1$ advances clockwise to $1:e_2$, whose paired dart is $2:e_2$. The five displayed cycles are disjoint and contain all twelve darts, so they are exactly the face cycles. Hence $V=3$, $E=6$, $F=5$, and $V-E+F=2$. [given, F1, step 2.1, construct]\n\n3.1 Each edge occurs at its two ends once incoming and once outgoing by the six directed segments in step 2.1. Each crossing record has its two opposite oriented strands, the over/under designation, and the positive cyclic order from [F1]–[F2]. Traversing from $1:U_{\\rm out}$ follows $x_1$ to $3:O_{\\rm in}$, then the opposite over-dart follows $x_2$ to $2:U_{\\rm in}$, then follows $x_3$ to $1:O_{\\rm in}$, then $x_4$ to $3:U_{\\rm in}$, then $x_5$ to $2:O_{\\rm in}$, and finally $x_6$ to $1:U_{\\rm in}$; the opposite under-dart there is the starting $1:U_{\\rm out}$. This single cycle covers all six visits, and the edge graph connects crossings $1,2,3$. By [F3], the Euler count gives the spherical realization; choosing $F_0$ yields the selected plane model. [given, F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, step 1.2, construct]\n\n4.1 The code therefore passes the validator's ordered checks, and [F4] returns its diagram with these face cycles and outer face. Its normalized crossing signs, edge pairing, and selected cycle occur in the enumerator at $c=3$; the one-cycle component trace passes the knot filter. By [F5], the reconstructed candidate is included at bound $3$. [given, F4, F5, step 3.1] ∎"
    }
  ]
